course: dictionary
lesson: 1655
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Many words have more than one meaning, and chip is one of them. You cannot tell which meaning is right from the word alone. You need the clues in the sentence around it. Ask: what is this sentence about: food, a computer, a pet, or a broken mug?
explain: «Anyone who has eaten chips on a seaside promenade knows the gull that swoops down.» Eating and seaside tell you that chips here are food. In the UK, chips are long pieces of fried potato, so the clue is what people do with them, not what the word looks like.
explain: «Most microchips carry a 15-digit number that no other animal shares.» The words number, animal and microchip are your clues. A microchip is a tiny device put inside a pet, and it holds an ID number. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it fits.
explain: «Inside your phone is a computer chip smaller than a fingernail.» Phone and computer are the clues here. A chip is a tiny piece of material with circuits that makes electronic things work. The same word, three different meanings, and the context picks the right one.
fact: Some words have several meanings. Look at the sentence around the word to find which one is right.
fact: «Chip» can mean a small piece broken off something hard, such as wood, stone or a plate.
fact: «Chips» can mean long fried potatoes in the UK, Ireland, Australia and NZ, or thin crunchy potato slices from a packet in the US and Canada.
fact: A «computer chip» is a tiny piece of material with circuits that makes phones, tablets and calculators work.
fact: To «chip» can also mean to kick or hit a ball gently into the air.
exercise: «Anyone who has eaten chips on a seaside promenade knows the gull that swoops down.» What are the chips in this sentence? | A small piece of wood broken off a fence | A tiny computer part that makes phones work | A small mark left on a mug | *Long pieces of fried potato, eaten at the seaside
exercise: «Most microchips carry a 15-digit number that no other animal shares.» Where might a microchip with a number like this be found? | On a chipped mug in the kitchen | On the edge of a plate | Inside a bag of crisps | *Inside a pet, such as a cat or dog
exercise: «Inside your phone is a computer chip smaller than a fingernail.» What does chip mean here? | *A tiny piece of material with circuits that makes electronic things work | A thin crunchy slice of potato from a packet | A small mark where a piece broke off | A counter used in board games
test: Mum says, «That mug is chipped, so throw it away, it could cut you.» What does chipped mean? | Painted bright red | *Has a small piece broken off | Very clean and shiny | Full of hot tea
test: Ruby from Texas says, «Grab a bag of chips for the trip.» Her chips are thin crunchy potato slices from a packet. What would people in the UK call them? | *Crisps | Wafers | Fries | Biscuits
exam: «Kieran competes with Sam at everything: who finished his chips first.» What are the chips in this sentence? | Computer parts that make phones work | *Long pieces of fried potato, as in fish and chips | Small marks left on a mug | Chocolate chips in a cookie
